smoke-tutor: Because both entries match the destination, but they match at different levels of specificity [module]. The entry `10.0.2.0/24` fixes the first 24 bits, which exactly match the first 24 bits of `10.0.2.9`, so it is a precise match. The default route `0.0.0.0/0` fixes zero bits, so it matches every possible destination, including `10.0.2.9`, but it is the least specific entry [module].

The rule is that the most specific matching prefix wins [module]. So `10.0.2.9` takes `10.0.2.0/24 -> 10.0.9.2` rather than the default route. The default route is only used when no more specific entry matches [module].
smoke-tutor: Think of the default route as the “everything else” route: `0.0.0.0/0` fixes zero bits, so it matches any address [module]. But `10.0.2.0/24` is more specific — it matches addresses that start with `10.0.2` [module].

Since `10.0.2.9` starts with `10.0.2`, the router uses the `10.0.2.0/24` row. The default route is only used when no more specific row matches [module].
